Lambda bodies are **out of line, after the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:521:   emitted first, then bodies are dequeued and emitted one at a time,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:522:   each a contiguous range ending in `:return`; bodies discovered while\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:583:   into contiguous bodies: the main sequence at 0 and one body per\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:634:    differ, `:noncanonical-layout` when bodies or labels are placed\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:637:    would accept a vector with two bodies swapped, which is not the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:958:4. **Layout.** Bodies and pcs keep A's layout; the register image's\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:959:   `{:bodies … :instructions …}` shape is filled from the body ranges.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:973:   conditionals, `:define name` after its value, bodies out of line in\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:999:their pinned outcomes and the eight module bodies run as root programs,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:1056:until phase 8.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:1074:   `load-vector` returns is `{:vector :address :bodies [{:start :end\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:1080:4. **Evaluator.** `yin.vm.semantic-register` beside `yin.vm.semantic`.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:1082:   `:yin.semantic-register/code`, contract `\"v4\"`; phase 5 supplies its\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:1112:   then bodies FIFO; a definition visits only its value operand), on the\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:1163:   `yin.vm.semantic-register` to `yin.vm.semantic`, remove the old H/R\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:1183:   sources and the v3 halves of `yin.vm.ucf` died in 8f-1 rather than\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:1188:   digest everywhere in yin.vm except SHA-256 explicitly minted in\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:34:`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`, beside `yin.vm.semantic`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:37:`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/` (`code`, `linearize`, `analysis`)\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:68:| `:telemetry` `:telemetry-step` `:telemetry-t` `:telemetry-eid` `:vm-model` `:vm-id` | telemetry | same; `:vm-model :semantic` (`:semantic-register` until the 2026-10-15 cutover) | reused |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:213:| shape | `{:segment seg :length n :code [[opcode-int …] …] :address A}` | `{:segment seg :length n :vector v :address A :bodies [{:start :end :owner :registers} …] :analysis a}` |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:216:| `:vector` `:address` `:bodies` | — | exactly what `code/load-vector` returns (`code.cljc:864-879`), `:end` exclusive |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:857:2. `yin.vm.semantic.corpus/programs` (`test/yin/vm/semantic_register/corpus.cljc:54-118`):\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:911:- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc` (ns `yin.vm.semantic`,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:926:- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/vm_test.cljc` (ns `yin.vm.semantic.vm-test`).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:927:- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/walker_trace_test.cljc` (ns `yin.vm.semantic.walker-trace-test`).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:928:- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/parity_test.cljc` (ns `yin.vm.semantic.parity-test`).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:999:3. A loaded image holds `:vector :address :bodies :analysis :segment\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:1080:20. `clojure -M:kondo --lint src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc test/yin/vm/semantic_register` reports no errors.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:1090:- `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`: the remaining arms\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:1096:- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/effects_test.cljc` (ns `yin.vm.semantic.effects-test`).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:1097:- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/link_test.cljc` (ns `yin.vm.semantic.link-test`).\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:1106:  `:semantic-register`), and `yin.vm.linker-require-test/backends`\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:1114:- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/walker_trace_test.cljc` and\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:1278:found no defect in `semantic_register.cljc` and 14 of 19 mutants caught\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:1503:> **Evaluator.** `yin.vm.semantic-register` beside `yin.vm.semantic`.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:1508:> `:yin.semantic-register/code`, contract `\"v4\"`; phase 5 supplies its\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md:1551:1. The interim link format is spelled `:yin.semantic-register/code`,\n# The canonical instruction vector contract -- revision history\n\nRevision: 1 (2026-09-25) -- architecture-owner publication of the\nhistory and the static safepoint-kind ruling in section 8.\n\nStatus: authoritative revision history for the `v2` stamp in\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc:38`. It completes the publication indexed by\n`yin.vm.semantic.md` section 2.4 (\"semantic.md\" below), closing the\ndocumentation part of UCF section 7.11's contract-revision-publication\nblocker and the Phase 1 sign-off's P2 publication gate. This ruling does\nnot accept UCF as a whole; UCF 7.11's other gates and tests remain.\n(2026-10-15: the stamp carrier and the \"semantic.md\" referent are both\ndeleted — entry I-6 below records the phase-8 cutover; the file name\n`yin.vm.semantic.md` now belongs to the register-shaped design, and this\ndocument is the authority for the historical `\"v2\"`/`\"v3\"` contracts.)\n\nQuotes below are verbatim except that section marks are written `S`\nand em dashes `--`, so every line here stays ASCII.\n\n## 1. Purpose, referent, and validity rule\n\nThe `v2` stamp (`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc:38-47`,\n`{:yin.code/contract \"v2\", :yin.k/version 0}`) names one published\nrevision of the complete execution contract. UCF section 7.3.3 lists\nwhat one revision names, and `yin.vm.semantic.md` (\"semantic.md\"\nbelow) section 2.4's \"The `\"v2\"` execution contract, published\" note\nis the index that gives each component its home. This file publishes\nthe history of that named contract: per revision, what changed, which\nsections it amended, the commit or artifact that carried it, and\nwhether already-published images remain valid under it.\n\nValidity is judged by UCF's own contract-version semantics:\n\n- An engine's code index is keyed per stamp:\n  `(get-in index [stamp address])`; the same address under different\n  stamps is a different image (UCF 7.3.3).\n- \"Two revisions may be declared compatible only by a published\n  statement in `yin.vm.semantic.md` S2.4, never by an engine's own\n  judgment\" (UCF 7.3.3).\n- semantic.md section 2.4 declares no cross-revision compatibility.\n  A form-level change needs a new contract name if images exist; none\n  existed for D-0 through D-3. Later amendments below are classified\n  by whether they change a named component or only its carrier.\n- The UCF top warning stands \"until each of these is closed with a\n  test in the parity or conformance suite\" (UCF 7.11).\n\nScope notes. (a) The UCF sections cited live in\n`docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` since commit\n3325815d extracted them from semantic.md; semantic.md section 7 is a\npointer note. (b) The stamp's referent is the components of UCF 7.3.3\n/ semantic.md 2.4 (section 2 here); revisions to other UCF sections\nare listed only when they touch the code-identity section 7.3, or\nmarked as outside the referent.\n\n## 2. What one revision names (the referent)\n\nPer semantic.md section 2.4, the `v2` contract is, by reference:\n\n- Mnemonic set: the section 2.4 opcode table's `:yin.code/op` column,\n  21 values, matching `yin.vm.code/mnemonics`.\n- Per-mnemonic arity and operand kinds: the section 2.4 \"Operand\n  attributes\" column; enforced by section 2.6 rule 2\n  (`:instruction-shape`) and rule 5 (`:dangling-target`).\n- Saturation/defaults: semantic.md 2.4 \"Saturation and defaults\",\n  corrected by section 7.5 below for `:call`'s absent tail flag\n  and the unreachable `:ffi-call` argc fallback.\n- Opcode table and transitions: semantic.md 2.4 \"Opcode integers\" and\n  the section 4.2 transition equations.\n- Resolution rule: section 4.2's rho = `engine/resolve-var`,\n  env -> store -> primitives -> module registry.\n- Last-value-wins rule: a repeated single-valued instruction\n  attribute keeps its last value in datom order, as `code.cljc`'s\n  `index-batch` and `yin.vm/index-datoms` both implement it.\n- Effect outcome map: semantic.md section 3.3's table.\n- Scheduler semantics: semantic.md section 3.5.\n\nLoad-bearing but inherited, not named by the stamp: the address basis\n`(dao.jing/segment-key vector)`. UCF 7.3.2: \"UCF must not mint a\nsecond addressing scheme\". Both the default digest and the byte\nencoding beneath `segment-key` changed before the Phase 1 stamp\nlanded (I-0 and I-1 below). UCF's `:segment/sha256-...` examples\npredate the digest change; current implicit minting uses BLAKE3.\n\n## 3. Design-phase revisions (before UCF execution)\n\nNo UCF-stamped image existed in this period, so stamped-image validity\nis vacuous. D-0 did have Phase 0 executable code; it did not implement\nUCF addressing. D-1 and D-2 are known only as named predecessors in\nUCF 7.3.2; their artifacts are absent from `collab/` (section 7.3).\n\nD-0. The reservation (semantic.md 2.2). `:yin.code/hash` is reserved:\n\"Reserved: content address of the canonical instruction datoms, for\ndedup and verification (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` S6.3). Not\nrequired in Phase 1.\" Carrier: semantic.md as promoted from the\nPhase 0 findings note (collab/1789221648668-architect-semantic-\nvm-v2-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md, absent from `collab/`);\nin-tree from commit 27fc0f9a (2026-09-13).\nSections amended: none -- first statement. UCF-stamped images: none.\n\nD-1. The first draft (2026-09-14). Canonical form: sorted `[e a v]`\ntriples over the batch. UCF 7.3.2's verdict, quoted: \"the first\ndraft's sorted `[e a v]` triples, which were neither total nor\nexecution-faithful\" -- constants arriving `1, 2` versus `2, 1` sorted\nidentically while executing differently, the collision the review\ndemonstrated. Carrier: the 2026-09-14 first draft; no in-tree commit\ncarries this text (the earliest committed UCF text already postdates\nit). Sections amended: UCF 7.3.2 (then section 7.3 of the semantic.md\nappendix). Images: none existed.\n\nD-2. The r2 revision (2026-09-14). Canonical form: the attributed-map\nrecord `{pc -> {attr -> value}}`, answering findings 1-19 of the\narchitectural review\n(`collab/1789387292000-architect-ucf-review.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`,\nabsent from `collab/`). UCF 7.3.2's verdict, quoted: \"sound but\ncarried datom vocabulary into the identity layer -- attribute names\nand admissibility rules where position and the opcode table suffice\".\nCarrier: the 2026-09-14 r2 revision; no in-tree commit carries this\ntext. Sections amended: UCF 7.3.2. Images: none existed.\n\nD-3. The r3 owner ruling (2026-09-14). Canonical form: the\npositional instruction tuple vector -- one tuple per instruction, pc\nis the index, the semantic.md 2.4 table fixes each mnemonic's tuple\narity and operand kinds, nothing else names a slot. Two owner rulings\nare recorded in UCF 7.3.2: tuples are the correct way to represent a\nsemantic AST, though the datom `[e a v t m]` need not be the tuple\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git show HEAD:docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | head -125; rg -n 'Revision|v3|v2' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md | tail -22; sed -n '1,100p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1,85p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md; sed -n '1,100p' collab/1792050511876-architect-8f1-ruling.report.md; sed -n '693,740p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc; rg -n 'index-from-datoms|address-attribute|publish\"'!|identity-fn'\"' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc; cat src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc; sed -n '125,148p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/frame.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git show HEAD:docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md | head -125; rg -n 'Revision|v3|v2' docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md | tail -22; sed -n '1,100p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md; sed -n '1,85p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md; sed -n '1,100p' collab/1792050511876-architect-8f1-ruling.report.md; sed -n '693,740p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc; rg -n 'index-from-datoms|address-attribute|publish\"'!|identity-fn'\"' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker.cljc; cat src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc; sed -n '125,148p' src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/frame.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"# yin.vm.semantic on dao.stream — linear executable datoms\n\nRevision: 2 (2026-09-25), Rule R: the execution contract this document\nnames is `\"v3\"`, published in full by the S2.4 contract note and its\nrevision history, per the UCF design's S7.11 contract-revision-publication\nblocker (`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`). Revision 1\n(2026-09-18; status corrected 2026-09-25) published `\"v2\"`; `\"v3\"` adds\n`:define` and the reserved name `yin/def`. The revision history of the\n`\"v2\"` contract is in `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` (2026-09-25).\n\nStatus: Phase 0 contract. Sections §1–§6 are promoted verbatim from\n`collab/1789221648668-architect-semantic-vm-v2-design.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md`.\nThe opcode table (§2.4) and the attribute tables (§2.2, §2.3) are the\ncontract that `yin.vm/code-schema`, `yin.vm/opcode-table`, and\n`yin.vm.code/well-formed?` implement. The integration and roadmap\nsections remain in the findings note; §8 records the Phase 4 measurements.\n\n---\n\n## §1. Foundational axioms and invariant compliance\n\n### 1.1 The four axioms\n\n**Everything is a Stream.** Code arrives on a program stream as a segment\nvalue; execution is consumption of that stream by an observer, and then\nconsumption of the segment by the machine. Effects the program performs\n(`:stream-put`, `:stream-next`, FFI) are emissions on streams the composition\nsupplied, exactly as for the walker. Optional execution traces are emissions\ntoo (§3.6). What is *not* a stream is the instruction fetch inside a loaded\nsegment, and this is deliberate: a boundary belongs \"wherever you need\ndecoupling — and nowhere else\" (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §1). Fetch has\none consumer, one order, no replay need, and no host boundary; putting a\nstream there is the per-value tax §4 of that note names as hopeless.\n\n**Interpretation Creates Semantics.** `[e :yin.code/op :call]` means nothing\nuntil `yin.vm.semantic` reads it. The same datoms can be read by a query\n(\"all call sites of this closure\"), a renderer (back to source through\n`:yin.code/source`), a verifier (every block ends in a terminator), or a\ndifferent VM. The mapping from the keyword mnemonic in the datom to the\ninteger opcode in the image is the loader's interpretation, not a fact in\nthe datom. `:yin.code/tail?` is a property of a call in the datoms; only the\nimage decides it is a distinct `tailcall` dispatch.\n\n**Code and State are Datoms.** Instructions are entities with attributes.\nExecution state is plain data: the control is `{:segment id :pc n}`, the\nenvironment a map, the operand stack a vector, the continuation a vector of\nframes each `{:pc :env :stack-base :segment}`. The optional trace vocabulary\n(§3.6) deposits exactly these as `[e a v t m]` with `t` the step counter,\nthe \"endgame\" of `cesk-space-optimization.md` §6: execute fast, emit the\nmachine image as datoms rather than interpret from them.\n\n**Everything is a Continuation.** Park writes `{segment pc env stack k}` into\n`:parked`; resume reads it back. Nothing in that map is a host object except\nvalues the program itself put in `env` or on the stack (host functions\nresolved from primitives, stream handles from the store), which is the same\nserialization limit the walker has today. A continuation shipped through a\nstream names its segment by id; the receiving host resolves the id against\nsegment datoms it holds or was sent alongside.\n\n### 1.2 The six invariants\n\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Invariant                | How the design honors it                                                         |\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| No hidden global state   | Opcode table is a value in `yin.vm`. Segments live in the VM record under     |\n|                          | `:code`. Primitives, modules, `:make-stream`, bridge handlers are all supplied   |\n|                          | at construction, as for the walker. No `defonce`, no registry.                   |\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| No implicit control flow | Every transfer of control is an instruction datom: `:jump`, `:branch-false`,     |\n|                          | `:call`, `:return`, `:halt`. Sequencing is the explicit `:yin.code/pc` order. No |\n|                          | fallthrough that is not a `pc + 1` visible in the datoms.                        |\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| No callbacks             | Host effects go through `engine/handle-effect` and the FFI request/response      |\n|                          | pair; a blocked read parks in the polling wait set. The VM invokes nothing and   |\n|                          | is invoked by nothing.                                                           |\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| No shared mutable state  | The image array is built once per segment and never written after load. The hot  |\n|                          | loop keeps registers in `loop` locals; the persistent VM record is the only      |\n|                          | state that escapes a step. No mutable deftype fields (a cljd trap on record).    |\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| No layer collapsing      | Lowering (AST datoms → code datoms), loading (code datoms → image), and          |\n|                          | execution (image → transitions) are three functions in two namespaces with data  |\n|                          | between each. The walker keeps interpreting the AST; nothing in the walker       |\n|                          | changes.                                                                         |\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| No assumed graphs        | Branch targets and closure bodies are refs; the loader constructs the pc index   |\n|                          | from tuples in an explicit pass and resolves refs against it. A dangling ref is  |\n|                          | a load error naming the entity, never a runtime lookup.                          |\n+--------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n---\n\n## §2. The linear executable datom specification\n\n### 2.1 Tuple format\n\nExecutable datoms are canonical 5-tuples `[e a v t m]`, produced the same\nway `yin.vm/ast->datoms` produces AST datoms: negative tempids for `e`,\n`t` = 0 unless the linearizer is given one, `m` = `dao.datom/default-op`.\nAttributes live under the `:yin.code/*` namespace so that\n`engine/executable-program-datom?` (which selects `:yin/*`) does not confuse\na code segment with an AST program, and a query can select either view.\n\n### 2.2 The segment\n\nA segment is one entity plus its instructions. It is the unit a program\nstream carries (one batch value), the unit the loader accepts, and the unit\na closure or continuation names.\n\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Attribute                | Value              | Meaning                                                                          |\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:yin.code/type`         | `:segment`         | Marks the segment entity.                                                        |\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:yin.code/length`       | int                | Number of instructions; pcs are `0 .. length-1`, dense.                          |\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:yin.code/derived-from` | ref (AST root eid) | Provenance; optional when hand-assembled.                                        |\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| `:yin.code/hash`         | segment address    | Reserved: content address of the segment's canonical instruction vector          |\n|                          |                    | (`dao.jing/segment-key`; UCF draft §7.3), for dedup and verification             |\n|                          |                    | (`streams-all-the-way-down.md` §6.3). Not required in Phase 1.                   |\n+--------------------------+--------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\nEntry is pc 0 by rule. Making entry a fact would let a segment disagree with\nitself; a rule cannot.\n243:validity: phase 1 is the first carrier of the `v2` stamp, so there\n273:record with `:contract \"v2\"`: \"a link request for this format\n284:I-4. Rule R: the contract moves to v3 (0fc931fc, 2026-09-26 05:06:02\n292:\"v3\", `semantic-contract` \"v3\", `stack-contract` \"b2\",\n294:carries `{:yin.code/contract \"v3\", :yin.k/version 0}` through\n303:images stamped \"v2\", \"b1\" or \"r1\" no longer load and there is no\n306:the same rule for manifests: a manifest naming \"v2\", \"b1\" or \"r1\" is\n359:  - Not changed: code stamps \"v3\", \"b2\", \"r2\", the vector grammar,\n408:    `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md` and its\n500:    Revision: 1 (2026-09-18) -- the `\"v2\"` execution contract this\n501:    document names is published in full by S2.4's \"v2 contract\n513:    P2, Phase 2 gate | ucf.cljc:38 | The `v2` stamp names a contract\n534:findings note (collab/1789221648668-architect-semantic-vm-v2-design\n553:vector (`ucf.cljc:77-78`). This is existing `v2` behavior, not a new\n559:unreachable for well-formed `v2` input. The Phase 1 reviewer flagged\n596:a naming correction to the Phase 1 safepoint API, not a new `v2`\n647:Second, the `\"v3\"` canonicalizer and the stack-shaped safepoint halves\n649:of the v3 batch survived 8e, and the register machine's validator,\n652:only `code-address`; the `v2` stamp carrier this document's header\n660:(c), phase-8 plan) and the stack-shaped v3 design (\"yin.vm.semantic on\n662:`\"v2\"` then `\"v3\"`) was deleted, its §1 stance and this revision\n672:7.4.1-7.4.3, 7.11); the deleted v3 `yin.vm.semantic.md` (header, 2.2,\n# yin.vm.semantic.continuation: phase 6, the continuation format and the row carrier\n\nStatus: **Companion design, phase 6 of `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n§10.** Written 2026-10-11 (Architect: claude-fable-5-1) from the frozen\ndesign as amended (`8f0b9e38`), the landed 4a evaluator\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`) and the 4b stage-1 evaluator\non branch `srvm-phase4b` (read only), the merged phase-5 derivations\ndesign, the UCF document and its version-2 amendment, the row-carrier\ndraft (`yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md`, body version 3, never landed),\nthe two de Bruijn kernels, and the landed UCF code and harness\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc`, `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc`,\n`test/yin/vm/ucf/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`). Revision 2 (2026-10-11)\napplies the codex review\n`collab/1791760000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(CHANGES_REQUESTED): the task's free environment is carried explicitly\n(§4.4, §6.3), the stack kernel's K slices and tail completion are\ncorrected and gated (§4.3, §8.3), register return destinations are\nrecovered from A (§4.2), `act` and `tail` have separate algorithms, the\nvalidator has a complete pure input contract and the carrier's order\n(§3), the static laws use actual-successor live-out (§5.6), blinding is\na specified re-emission (§5.5), the harness matrix is expanded (§5.2),\n6a is the independent codec (§8.1), and the amendment inventory is\nexhaustive (§10). Revision 3 (2026-10-11) applies the codex round-2\nreview\n`collab/1791800000000-architect-srvm-phase6-design-review-r2.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(CHANGES_REQUESTED): stack lowering bases are cumulative through each\nslice (§4.3), the live operand subtracts the virtual destination before\nmapping (§4.2, §5.6), the free environment's source on lift is specified\nwith its owner (§6.3), E2 requires lexical presence and X1 validates the\nroot's free environment (§3.2), the harness oracles of rows 15, 20, 22\nand 23 and the segment-wrapper results are corrected (§5.2), the\ncross-lane re-emission takes an explicit stream correspondence (§5.5),\nand the amendment residue is closed (§10.3 R9, U7). **Revision 4\n(2026-10-13, Architect: claude-fable-5-1)** amends the text after slice\n6b merged (`1db08f7d`: `c3d37586` plus the fixes `a396f996`, `f650e046`,\n`bafdec74`), so that §§2.5-2.6, 5.2, 6.1, 6.3, 7, 8.2 state what\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (`lift-task`, `lower-task`,\n`check-stores!`, `check-pendings!`, `check-dependencies!`,\n`check-custody!`, `discharge!`, `live-pcs`) does; every amended clause\nis marked \"**Amended after 6b**\" with what changed and why, §10.6\ninventories them, and §11 lists the design choices the code made that\nthe owner has not ruled on. Sources: the engineer report\n`collab/1792270000000-engineer-6b-task-transport.report.md` (design bugs\nB1-B4, deviations 1-10) and the three codex reviews\n(`collab/1792280000000-…`, `1792300000000-…`, `1792320000000-…`). The\nfrozen document is not changed by this one. **Revision 5 (2026-10-13,\nArchitect: claude-fable-5-1)** amends the text after slice 6c merged\n(`781a9ba8`: `47ddb1d9` plus the fix round `dc545aaa`, `30730c51`), so\nthat §§4.1-4.3, 5.2, 5.3, 5.5, 5.6, 6.3, 7, 8.3, 8.5, 9 state what\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/layout.cljc` (`segment`, `space`,\n`phys`, `virt`, `lift-state`, `lower-state`, `canonical-task`,\n`kernel-task`, `lower-defect`, `lift-task`, `lower-task`) and the 6c\ntests (`layout_test.cljc`, the harness's cross-engine lanes,\n`store_write_audit_test.clj`) do; every amended clause is marked\n\"**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13)**\" with what changed and why, §10.7\ninventories them, and §11 gains the three questions slice 6c left for\nthe owner, one of them answered. Sources: the\nengineer report `collab/1792340000000-engineer-6c-layouts.report.md`\n(deviations 1-8, design defects D1-D5), the codex review\n`collab/1792350000000-review-6c-layouts.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md` (F1-F6,\nD1-D5) and the Architect's rulings\n`collab/1792360000000-architect-6c-rulings.report.md` (R1-R6), whose\nwording is applied verbatim where it says so. **Revision 6 (2026-10-13,\nArchitect: claude-fable-5-1)** corrects every clause that called the de\nBruijn kernels, the native lanes or their refusals short-lived: by the\nowner's ruling of 2026-10-13 the two de Bruijn kernels are **backends**\n(lowerings of the semantic bytecode, A to H and A to R), are\nnot deleted at phase 8, support a documented fragment that is not\nwidened, and refuse before any attach what they cannot represent (§4.6);\neach corrected clause is marked \"**Amended 2026-10-13 (backends)**\", 6c\nQ1's rationale is restated and 6c Q2 is closed as rejected (§11).\nSubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md), including its\nunitarity invariant, and to\n[`yin.vm.semantic.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.md)\n(\"the frozen design\"); format and rigor model:\n[`yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md)\nand [`yin.vm.semantic.derivations.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.derivations.md).\n\nEvery section marks its content **DECIDED** (an owner ruling, frozen\ntext, or code that exists) or **PROPOSED** (a design choice of this\ndocument, open to the reviewer). File references are to the worktree at\n`8f0b9e38`; line numbers are those of that commit; the 4b branch is\ncited as `srvm-phase4b:semantic_register.cljc`, with the line numbers of\nits stage-1 commit (`0f4522b9`) unless a citation says stage 2\n(`bdf48cf4`, committed after revision 2; stage 2 moved the reified\npayload to `:256`, `create-vm` to `:349-375` and `gc-roots`/`gc-children`\nto `:485-488`, and changed none of the shapes this document reads).\n\nContents: §0 rulings and the one-paragraph answer · §1 the canonical\nframe · §2 the row carrier · §3 the frame validator · §4 layouts · §5\nthe safepoint harness · §6 code and task context in the carrier · §7\nlift and lower · §8 slices, gates and phase 7's entry conditions · §9\nverification honesty · §10 findings and amendments · §11 owner\nquestions · §12 portability.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Rulings and the one-paragraph answer\n\n### 0.1 Rulings that bind this design (DECIDED)\n# yin.vm.semantic.evaluator: the phase-4 evaluator, grounded in the engine\n\nStatus: **Companion design, phase 4 of `yin.vm.semantic.md`\n§10.** Written 2026-10-10 (Architect: claude-fable-5-1) from the frozen\ndesign, the engine as it stands at `d21ee43f`, and the landed phase-3\ncode (`f46935ad`). The frozen document is not changed by this one: where\nthe engine's reality differs from its text, §6 records a finding with a\nproposed amendment. Subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md)\nand to [`yin.vm.semantic.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.md)\n(\"the frozen design\" below). Revision 2 (2026-10-10) applies the\nindependent review `collab/1791540000000-architect-srvm-phase4-design-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n(CHANGES_REQUESTED): the trace oracle is defined by production versus\nrelocation and extracts destinations explicitly (§4), the slices share\none eligibility predicate and corrected counts (§5), the FFI-throw and\nstamp claims are restated (§2.2, §5.2), effect-boundary materialization\nis explicit (§1.8), and §6 holds the ruled amendment texts verbatim.\n\nEvery section marks its content **DECIDED** (follows from frozen text or\nfrom code that exists) or **PROPOSED** (a design choice of this\ndocument, open to the reviewer). File references are to the worktree at\n`d21ee43f`; line numbers are those of that commit.\n\nContents: §1 state shapes · §2 the restore helper · §3 link and install\n· §4 the walker trace test · §5 slices 4a/4b and gates · §6 findings and\nproposed amendments · §7 questions for the owner · §8 portability notes.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Concrete in-VM state shapes\n\n### 1.1 Where the evaluator lives\n\n**DECIDED.** Namespace `yin.vm.semantic`, file\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc`, beside `yin.vm.semantic`\n(frozen §10 phase 4 names it so; §10 phase 8 renames it). It requires\nthe three landed phase-3 namespaces under\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/` (`code`, `linearize`, `analysis`)\nand the same engine seams `yin.vm.semantic` requires\n(`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:35-44`): `yin.vm`, `yin.vm.engine`,\n`yin.vm.ffi`, `yin.vm.module`, `yin.vm.telemetry`, `yin.vm.ucf` (slice 4a\ndoes not need it: the image address comes from `code/load-vector`),\n`yin.vm.values`. It does **not** require `yin.vm.code` (the stack\noperand table) or `yin.vm.linearize` (the stack lowering).\n\n### 1.2 The machine state map\n\nThe engine treats a VM as a map and reads a fixed set of keys. The\ntable lists every key the stack-shaped `SemanticVM` record carries\n(`semantic.cljc:51-80`, plus the extension keys `vm/empty-state` adds,\n`src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:2053`), and what the register evaluator does with\neach.\n\n| Key | Stack VM today (`semantic.cljc`) | Register VM | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `:control` | `{:segment seg :pc pc}` or nil (`:107`) | same shape, same nil-when-halted rule | reused unchanged |\n| `:value` | the accumulator `val`, written back every exit (`:103`) | **not an accumulator.** The exit value only: the halt result, the parked record on `:park`, `:yin/blocked` when blocked (`engine/handle-stream-block` sets it, `engine.cljc:2329`) | extended in meaning |\n| `:stack` | the operand stack `St` (`:104`) | **gone.** Never present on the record | removed |\n| `:window` | — | `W`: a persistent map `{reg value}` for the current activation, body-local ids, sparse. **Always a map, never nil** (§8.4) | new |\n| `:env` | `E` (`:105`) | same; carries `engine/store-of-key` as today (`engine.cljc:177-202`) | reused unchanged |\n| `:k` | vector of frames or **nil when empty** (`:106`, because `engine/ready-for-ingress?` asks `(nil? (:k vm))`, `engine.cljc:280`) | same vector-or-nil rule; frames of §1.3 | reused, frame shape changed |\n| `:code` | `{seg {:segment :length :code :address}}` (`:684-689`, `:805-808`) | `{seg image}`, image = §1.7 | extended |\n| `:code-aliases` | `{address seg}` (`:711-724`) | same, keyed by `ucf/code-address` of the v4 vector | reused unchanged |\n| `:program` | last loaded segment id (`:747`) | same | reused unchanged |\n| `:halted?` `:blocked?` | engine flags | same meanings (`engine.cljc:245-261`) | reused unchanged |\n| `:parked` `:wait-set` `:ready-queue` `:id-counter` | scheduler tables | same tables; entry shapes of §1.4-1.6 | reused, entry payloads changed |\n| `:store` `:module-stores` `:resources` `:heap` `:gc` | engine-owned | same, untouched by the evaluator except through `engine/put-active`, `engine/handle-effect` | reused unchanged |\n| `:primitives` `:primitive-profiles` `:primitive-canonical-names` `:modules` `:make-stream` `:call-capacity` `:bridge` `:owner` `:capability-secret` `:secret-source` `:attach-stream` `:origin` `:origins` `:ancestry` `:installs` `:link-retired` `:link-diagnostics` `:ffi-caller-id` `:ffi-diagnostics` `:callable-effects` | composition and engine fields | same | reused unchanged |\n| `:telemetry` `:telemetry-step` `:telemetry-t` `:telemetry-eid` `:vm-model` `:vm-id` | telemetry | same; `:vm-model :semantic` (`:semantic-register` until the 2026-10-15 cutover) | reused |\n\n**DECIDED** (frozen §2.1): the configuration `⟨seg pc W E S K⟩` sits in\nthe record as `:control` (seg, pc), `:window` (W), `:env` (E), the\nstore keys (S), `:k` (K). The hot loop keeps `seg pc W E K` in `loop`\nlocals exactly as `vm-hot` keeps `seg pc val St E K` today\n(`semantic.cljc:284-292`) and writes them back through one\n`put-registers` (`semantic.cljc:90-107` is the template; its `val St`\nparameters become `W`, and `:value` is written only at exits). The\nderived-fact table (§8.1 of the frozen design) is read from the image\n(§1.7), never recomputed in the loop.\n\n**PROPOSED** record definition: a `defrecord RegisterVM` with the\n`SemanticVM` field list minus `stack` plus `window` (`semantic.cljc:51-80`).\nExtension keys (`:resources`, `:owner`, …) land in the record's\nextension map through `map->RegisterVM`, as they do for `SemanticVM`\ntoday (`semantic.cljc:937`). A record rather than a bare map keeps\n`extend-type` dispatch for `vm/IVM`, `vm/IVMState` and\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-15 07:52:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-15 14:52:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude (claude-fable-5-1)\nSession-ID: pending (claude -p; the harness does not expose it)\n\n# 8f-1 Architect ruling: who owns what `yin.vm.code` still provides once it is deleted\n\nTree: srvm-phase8f1 @ d43afbe3. Read-only; this report is the only artifact.\n\n## 0. What I verified (grep, not the engineer's word)\n\nRequirers on this base (`rg -l` over `src test`, require forms only):\n\n| doomed namespace | production requirers | test requirers |\n|---|---|---|\n| `yin.vm.code` | `debruijn_code.cljc`, `ucf.cljc`, `completion.cljc`, `semantic.cljc`, `ucf/handoff.cljc` | `code_test`, `debruijn_code_test`, `linearize_test`, `rule_r_test`, `dao/space/query_test` |\n| `yin.vm.semantic` | demos only (`datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff.cljc`, `demo/continuation_stream.cljs`, `yin/register_bench_cljd.cljd`) | 38 files (the plan's \"56 requirers\" set, incl. 9 C4 files, `test_utils`, `ucf_test`, `ucf/remote_test`) |\n| `yin.vm.linearize` | the same three demos | 36 files (largely the same set) |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.handoff` | nothing in src | `yang/python/antlr/int_heap_test.cljc:25` (one call, `handoff/export-task`, :240) |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` | `ucf/handoff.cljc` only | nothing |\n| `yin.vm.completion` | `ucf/handoff.cljc` only | `completion_test`, `cell_test`, `linker/publish_test`, `semantic_register/scan_test`, `debruijn_code_test` |\n| `yin.vm.ucf` | `semantic_register/scan.cljc:83`, `semantic_register/linearize.cljc:211,301` (both **`code-address` only**), plus `semantic.cljc:669`, `handoff.cljc`, `checkpoint.cljc` | `ucf_test` (24), `rule_r_test` (1 deftest), `semantic_register/{code,scan,linearize}_test` (`code-address` only) |\n\nFacts that change the ruling:\n\n1. **8d did not delete the old transport's sources.** Commit 249f8799 removed 8399 lines of tests and fixtures; `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc` (3783 lines) and `checkpoint.cljc` are still on disk, untested, with one C4 test caller. They are what keeps `yin.vm.completion` and the §7.3/§7.4 half of `yin.vm.ucf` \"live\". Their last commits are ae3fe21e / 49960017 (pre-phase-8). Plan Q2 said they die in 8d; they die in 8f-1 instead.\n2. **`yin.vm.completion` has no production reader except `handoff.cljc`.** `linker.cljc` never required it; the register-era lift is `engine/lift-slice` (`engine.cljc:1054`) and the rows carrier. So completion is dead production code the moment handoff goes.\n3. **Every production reader of `yin.vm.ucf` after (1) and (2) is `code-address`** (`jing/segment-key`). The §7.3 canonicalizer canonicalizes a v3 `:yin.code/*` batch, which nothing produces since 8e; the §7.4 safepoint half is stack-shaped (`:push` stack effects, positional `[:var name]`/`[:closure params body]`).\n4. **The descriptor is already pinned twice.** `debruijn_code_test/descriptor-declares-the-contract` pins `dc/descriptor-hash` = `7b7c6ae9…f3489`; `golden-corpus-bytes-and-hash` pins image bytes and H for four images; `debruijn_register_code_test/golden-descriptor-hash-test` pins `rcode/descriptor-hash` = `aa757659…7e74c` (routes through `debruijn-code/encode-scalar`, not through the stack opcode table). `encode-scalar` orders map entries by their encoded bytes, so a literal map with the same entries hashes identically to the derived one.\n5. **The native register backend's only dependency on `debruijn_code` is `encode-scalar`** (`debruijn_register_code.cljc:232-259`); `debruijn/stack.cljc:163` reads `dcode/opcode-table`. Neither touches `yin.vm.code`.\n6. **`vm/segment-requirements` + `segment-extraction-queries` (`vm.cljc:1839-1894`) are v3-positional** (`[:store-put ?key _]`, `[:ffi-call ?op _]`) and read only by `completion.cljc:258` and `dao/space/query_test.cljc:1865,1874`. `scan/requirements` (`scan.cljc:88-100`) is the v4 twin, with `scan/segment-rows` the v4 `project-segment-qualified` (as `derivations.md:901` already records).\n7. **`vm/opcode-table` (`vm.cljc:619`, the old stack opcode integers) is read only by `semantic.cljc` and `completion.cljc`**; the `opcase` macro carries its own literal copy and is used only by `semantic.cljc`. The optimisation note `src/cljc/yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md` mentions it (doc).\n8. **The v4 footprint `:mnemonics` table (`vm.cljc:1781-1797`) still has a `:push #{}` row.** Readers: `scan.cljc:76` (`dissoc :push`), `scan_test:254` (the same dissoc), `query_test:1838` (`(= code/mnemonics …)` against the old 22-mnemonic set).\n9. **\"v4 on v3\" acceptance exists at two sites, not one:** `semantic.cljc:795-796` (`load-vector` checks `vm/semantic-contract` then validates with the v3 `code/well-formed-vector?`) and `ucf.cljc:47,59,267-273` (`contract-stamp`/`profiles :semantic` stamp a v3 vector with `{:yin.code/contract \"v4\"}`; `canonicalize` checks the v4 stamp and emits a v3 vector).\n10. **Old `code/materialize-vector!` has no caller.** `linker_test`, `mint_test`, `linker_step_test`, `linker/publish.cljc:158` all call `yin.vm.semantic-register.code/materialize-vector!` (alias `code` there is the new ns; none of those files require `yin.vm.code`). `mint_test:256` tests the refusal-before-write. The only reference left is the docstring at `linker.cljc:2056-2057` and `dao/jing/content/driver.clj:8`.\n\n## 1. Rulings (DECIDED)\n\n### R1. The retained native stack grammar is owned by `yin.vm.debruijn-code`, as literal data, in place.\n\nInline the table: `opcode-table` becomes a literal map of the 23 mnemonics with their `[:yin.debruijn.code/<attr> kind]` slots, exactly the entries the derivation produces today (20 carried from the old table with the namespace moved, plus `:load-bound`, `:load-free`, `:closure`). Delete `renamespace-slots` and `carried-mnemonics`. Add `(def ^:private terminators #{:jump :return :halt})` beside it and point `terminator-rule` (:722) and `successors` (:749) at it. Drop the `[yin.vm.code :as code]` require. Rewrite the ns docstring lines 9-14 and the banner at :54-56 and :684 to say the table is this dimension's own data, frozen under `lowering-contract-version` 2.\n\nNo new namespace (`yin.vm.debruijn.stack-grammar` rejected: it would be a third place that spells the same 23 rows, and the descriptor already is the authoritative spelling, `:dim/slots opcode-table`). This is not \"ownership by invention\": the owner ruled the native kernels are retained backends with a documented fragment; a backend owns its own instruction grammar. Nothing is widened: same 23 mnemonics, same slots, same kinds.\n\n**Proof the bytes do not change** (codex runs before and after step 1, same JVM):\n\n- `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.debruijn-code-test` — `descriptor-declares-the-contract` must still pass with the literal `7b7c6ae92e5fe53577718b615a3ed0bdd81796bf8074efe8c07d7005c45f3489` **unchanged in the test file** (codex may not edit that literal); `golden-corpus-bytes-and-hash` must pass with its four `[bytes H]` pairs unchanged. Because `descriptor` embeds `opcode-table` and `mnemonic-tag` is derived from `(keys opcode-table)`, any slot, kind, attribute-name, or mnemonic drift flips both.\n- `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.debruijn-register-code-test` — `golden-descriptor-hash-test` literal `aa757659a28f9787e44cd04d02d9f4412eba1088d46a1b07adf9fa820d07e74c` unchanged.\n- At landing, the three-host lane (`pinned-identities-are-host-independent`, `register-determinism`), as every phase-8 slice.\n- Test rewrite: `opcode-table-derives-from-the-named-table` loses its first `testing` block (it compares to the deleted table). Replace it with: `(is (= 23 (count dc/opcode-table)))`, `(is (not (contains? dc/opcode-table :var)))`, every attribute namespaced `\"yin.debruijn.code\"`, every kind in `#{:data :sym :kw :str :bool :buffer :pc :uint}`; keep the second `testing` block verbatim. Rename it `opcode-table-is-this-dimensions-own-data`. Drop the `yin.vm.code` require from that test file.\n\n### R2. `yin.vm.ucf` shrinks to `code-address`. Its v3 halves die, un-ported.\n\nKeep `(defn code-address [v] (jing/segment-key v))` and a one-paragraph ns docstring (\"the A of a canonical vector; UCF mints no second addressing scheme\"). Delete everything else: `contract-stamp`, `profiles`, `supported-profiles`, `profile-engine`, the batch readers (`segment-attrs` … `check-segment-entity`, `canonical-tuple`, `batch->canonical-instruction-vector`, `canonicalize`, `refusal`), and the safepoint half (`transitions`, `parking-kinds`, `stack-effect`, `lexically-required`, `safepoints`, `safepoint-table`, `safepoint-at`, `activation-state`, `unsatisfied-names`). Requires drop to `dao.jing` only.\n\nWhy not port the validator/operand-table dependency to `semantic-register.code`: the §7.3 half reads a **v3 segment batch** (`:yin.code/op`, `:yin.code/pc`, saturation defaults for `[:call :yin.code/tail?]`); no producer of that batch survives 8e, and the register kernel's batch path is `semantic-register/linearize/project-datoms` + `code/well-formed?`. Porting would invent a v4 batch grammar nobody emits (derive-don't-persist). The safepoint half is stack-shaped; the register kernel's safepoint facts are already owned on the `semantic-register` side (`frame.cljc`, `semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test`, `frame_test`) and the rows carrier. Nothing is lost that is not stack-v3-specific.\n\nMoving `code-address` into `semantic-register.code` and deleting `ucf.cljc` was considered and rejected: `derivations.md:906` pins scan's imports to `yin.vm.ucf (code-address)`, three production sites and four test files spell `ucf/code-address`; the minimal diff keeps the namespace.\n\nTests: delete `test/yin/vm/ucf_test.cljc` whole (24 deftests; every fixture is a hand-assembled v3 batch run on `yin.vm.semantic`). Delete `rule_r_test/canonicalize-verifies-the-batch-stamp` (1 deftest). The stamp-verification fact survives in `rule_r_test/every-persistent-code-loader-requires-and-compares-a-stamp` once its \"semantic datoms\"/\"semantic vector\" legs are rewritten to the register kernel's loaders (8f-3, plan commit 2).\n\n### R3. `yin.vm.completion` is deleted with `handoff.cljc`. Nothing is re-derived.\n\n`completion.cljc` (757 lines) and `completion_test.cljc` (19 deftests) go. `vm.cljc` loses `segment-extraction-queries` and `segment-requirements` (:1839-1894) and `opcode-table` + `opcase` (:612-680; the `opcase` macro is only `semantic.cljc`'s). The docstring at `vm.cljc:1875-1886` (`ast-requirements`) drops its sentence about the segment twin.\n\nFacts that survive, and where:\n\n| fact completion_test / siblings asserted | survives as |\n|---|---|\n| tree and segment free names agree | `scan_test/assert-agreement` `(is (= (:free-names new) tree-names))` + `goldens-match` over `scanner_goldens.cljc` (historical v3 answers, master 993d5411) |\n| tree and segment requirement sets agree | `scan_test/assert-agreement` `(is (= (:requirements new) (vm/ast-requirements rows)))` |\n| lift refuses a cell | `cell_test/lift-of-a-bare-cell-ref-is-refused-test` (`engine/lift-slice`, on every VM); `int_heap_test/cell-refusal` blocks 2-3 (`engine/lift-slice`) |\n| image → vector round trip hashes to A | `semantic_register/load_paths_test`, `mint_test` (new kernel) |\n| FFI pair keys never in a slice | `debruijn_code_test:638` keeps the assertion with the set inlined |\n| module footprint discovery completes | **dropped**: `publish_test/published-footprint-completes-module-discovery` is a test of `completion/complete`; keep the test's first half (the published manifest carries `[:yin.module/footprint :effects]`) as an `is` on `manifest`, delete the completion half |\n| CLJS address-reuse fast path of the old query | **dropped** (`scan_test/completion-address-reuse-preserves-answers`, perf of deleted code) |\n\nEdits this implies (all compile-independent of the old kernel's deletion):\n\n- `semantic_register/scan_test.cljc`: drop requires `yin.vm.completion`, `yin.vm.linearize :as old-linearize`, and the `#?@(:cljd [] :cljs [[dao.jing] [dao.jing.cbor]])` block; delete `completion-free-names` (:131-176) and `completion-address-reuse-preserves-answers` (:266-270); in `assert-agreement` delete the `old-vector` binding and the `(completion-free-names old-vector)` term so the assertion reads `(is (= (:free-names new) tree-names))`.\n- `dao/space/query_test.cljc`: delete `segment-db` (:1826-1830) and its two uses: the second `is` of `definition-keys-are-store-slice-requirements` and the whole deftest `requirement-sets-from-tree-and-segment-are-equal` (the fact lives in `scan_test`, fact row 2). Drop the `yin.vm.code` and `yin.vm.linearize` requires; add `yin.vm.semantic-register.code :as sr-code` for R5.\n- `cell_test.cljc`: delete `completion-over-a-cell-is-not-complete-test` and the require; its `linearize`/`semantic` uses at :38 are 8f-3's.\n- `linker/publish_test.cljc`: as in the table.\n- `debruijn_code_test.cljc:638`: replace `completion/ffi-pair-keys` with `#{vm/call-in-stream-key vm/call-out-stream-key vm/call-out-cursor-key}`; drop the require.\n- `yang/python/antlr/int_heap_test.cljc`: delete the first `run-all` block of `cell-refusal` (:237-243) and the `yin.vm.ucf.handoff` require. C4 file: edited by this track under the phase-8 freeze (owner ruling 2026-10-14).\n- `store_write_audit_test.clj:339-350`: remove the `handoff.cljc` allowlist entry and its comment.\n\n### R4. `materialize-vector!`: the old one dies uncalled; the new one is the only one.\n\n`code/materialize-vector!` has zero callers; `semantic-register.code/materialize-vector!` (:881) is called by `linker/publish.cljc:158` and tested by `mint_test:117,138,256,274`, `linker_test:321,1153,1786`, `linker_step_test:453`. In 8f-1 fix the two stale prose references: `linker.cljc:2057` → `yin.vm.semantic-register.code/materialize-vector!` (becomes `yin.vm.semantic.code/…` in the 8f-2 rename; write the post-rename name only if 8f-2 lands first, else fix twice) and `dao/jing/content/driver.clj:8`. No test is added: the refusal-before-write fact is already `mint_test:256`.\n\n### R5. The \"v4 on v3\" acceptance dies at both sites; the footprint `:push` residue dies with it.\n\nSite 1, `semantic.cljc:795`, dies with the file (8f-3). Site 2, `ucf.cljc` stamps, dies in R2 (8f-1). Confirmation for the ledger: after 8f-1, `rg -n '\"v4\"|semantic-contract' src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc` returns nothing; after 8f-3, `rg -ln 'well-formed-vector\\?' src` returns only `semantic_register/code.cljc`.\n\n`:push`: delete the `:push #{}` row from `vm/footprint-table` (:1796), remove the `(dissoc … :push)` at `scan.cljc:76` and `scan_test:254` (rename that deftest `footprint-mnemonics-is-the-v4-table`), and make `query_test/footprint-table-has-a-row-for-every-tag-and-mnemonic` assert `(= sr-code/mnemonics (set (keys (:mnemonics table))))`. Readers of the `:push` row are exactly those three sites; the stack backend's `:push` is its own opcode, not a footprint fact.\n\n### R6. Docs do not move in 8f-1.\n\nThe statements that become historical (`debruijn.stack.md:211,510,644`; `derivations.md:119,901-914,1011`; `semantic-register-vm.md:153,981`; `linker.md:771-772,2332`; `universal-continuation-format.md:244`; `ucf-revisions.md` needs a dated entry that handoff/checkpoint sources died in 8f-1, not 8d; `dependency-completion.md` marked superseded by scan + lift-slice) are 8g's. Code docstrings that name a deleted var are fixed in the step that deletes it (kondo won't catch them; 8g's grep gate will).\n(def semantic-format\n  \"The production v4 semantic register vector format record.\"\n  {:format :yin.semantic/code\n   :contract sr-code/contract\n   :identity-fn jing/segment-key\n   :identity-matches-fn jing/segment-matches?\n   :row-defect-fn sr-code/well-formed?\n   :validate-fn sr-code/well-formed?\n   :obligations-fn scan/free-occurrences\n   :definitions-fn scan/definition-occurrences\n   :applications-fn scan/application-sites\n   :parts-fn (constantly nil)})\n\n\n(def ast-format\n  \"The `:yin.ast/code` format record: one row body `[tag & slots]` per\n   address (D3), the root row's id the tree's identity, under the \\\"v3\\\"\n   contract (`vm/ast-contract`). Step 2 is a bounded worklist over the\n   grammar's `node`/`nodes` slots; the whole tree is assembled for step\n   4 by prepending each part's address, the shape `validate-rows` takes.\n   All three scanners are Datalog over the rows, position-bearing by\n   occurrence path (section 5.1).\"\n  {:format              :yin.ast/code,\n   :contract            vm/ast-contract,\n   :identity-fn         jing/segment-key,\n   :identity-matches-fn jing/segment-matches?,\n   :row-defect-fn       row-local-defect,\n   :validate-fn         vm/validate-rows,\n   :obligations-fn      tree-free-name-occurrences,\n   :definitions-fn      tree-definition-occurrences,\n   :applications-fn     tree-application-sites,\n   :parts-fn            body-child-ids})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The manifest and derivation record formats (section 8.1)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def manifest-schema\n  \"The one module-manifest schema version this linker implements (section\n   8.1): which keys exist and what they mean. The manifest record's\n   validator implements exactly this version; a manifest of another\n   version is a `:schema` defect, never migrated.\"\n  1)\n\n\n(def ^:private manifest-keys\n  \"The closed key set of a schema-1 manifest: every key of section 8.1's\n664:   :identity-fn         debruijn-code/image-hash,\n682:   :identity-fn         debruijn-register-code/register-hash,\n697:   :identity-fn jing/segment-key\n717:   :identity-fn         jing/segment-key,\n882:   :identity-fn         jing/segment-key,\n899:   :identity-fn         jing/segment-key,\n944:(defn address-attribute\n951:(defn index-from-datoms\n957:  (let [attr (address-attribute format)]\n1240:   `publish!` and for refusal evidence, never by verification: step 3 is\n1244:  (try ((:identity-fn format) value)\n2051:(defn publish!\n2060:    [((:identity-fn format) image) (address-attribute format) address]))\n2290:                                     (= checksum ((:identity-fn record) image))))))\n(ns yin.vm.ucf\n  \"The A of a canonical vector: UCF mints no second addressing scheme.\n   Code identity delegates to dao.jing's canonical content address.\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]))\n\n\n(defn code-address\n  \"The dao.jing segment address of a canonical vector. UCF mints no second addressing scheme.\"\n  [v]\n  (jing/segment-key v))\n              (when-not (= {:yin.k/deliver :return} d) (problem :deliver [:yin.k/deliver]))))\n          (some (fn [[f p]]\n                  (let [fv (get code-map (:yin.k/segment f)) fi (nth fv (dec (:yin.k/pc f)))\n                        frd (:yin.k/rd f)]\n                    (or\n                      (when-not (and (= :call (first fi)) (not (code/tail-call? fi)))\n                        (problem :return-site (conj p :yin.k/pc)))\n                      (when-not (= frd (code/rd fi)) (problem :return-destination (conj p :yin.k/rd)))\n                      (window-defect f fv frd \"return-\" p)))) returns)\n          (some (fn [[f p]]\n                  (when (some vm/reserved-name? (keys (get-in f [:yin.k/env :yin.k/bindings])))\n                    (problem :reserved-name (conj p :yin.k/env :yin.k/bindings)))) envs)\n          (some (fn [[f p]]\n                  (let [names (lexical-names (get code-map (:yin.k/segment f)) (:yin.k/pc f))\n                        bindings (get-in f [:yin.k/env :yin.k/bindings])]\n                    (when-not (and (every? #(contains? bindings %) names)\n                                   (= (apply dissoc bindings names)\n                                      (apply dissoc (:free-env task) names)))\n                      (problem :free-env-mismatch (conj p :yin.k/env :yin.k/bindings))))) envs)\n          (when-not (and (contains? #{:wait :captured :parked} context)\n                         (= (= :wait context) (contains? frame :yin.k/pending)))\n            (problem :pending-presence [:yin.k/pending]))\n          (when (= :wait context)\n            (let [reasons (case op\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport subprocess,pathlib,difflib\nfor suffix in ['', '.continuation','.derivations','.evaluator','.cursor-cell-numbering']:\n old=subprocess.check_output(['git','show',f'HEAD:docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm{suffix}.md'],text=True)\n old=old.replace('yin.vm.semantic-register-vm','yin.vm.semantic').replace('yin.vm.semantic-register','yin.vm.semantic')\n new=pathlib.Path(f'docs/design/yin.vm.semantic{suffix}.md').read_text()\n print(''.join(difflib.unified_diff(old.splitlines(True),new.splitlines(True),fromfile='normalized HEAD'+suffix,tofile='working'+suffix)))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport subprocess,pathlib,difflib\nfor suffix in ['', '.continuation','.derivations','.evaluator','.cursor-cell-numbering']:\n old=subprocess.check_output(['git','show',f'HEAD:docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm{suffix}.md'],text=True)\n old=old.replace('yin.vm.semantic-register-vm','yin.vm.semantic').replace('yin.vm.semantic-register','yin.vm.semantic')\n new=pathlib.Path(f'docs/design/yin.vm.semantic{suffix}.md').read_text()\n print(''.join(difflib.unified_diff(old.splitlines(True),new.splitlines(True),fromfile='normalized HEAD'+suffix,tofile='working'+suffix)))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"--- normalized HEAD\n+++ working\n@@ -1,14 +1,41 @@\n # yin.vm.semantic: the canonical projection rebuilt register-shaped\n \n-Status: **Design draft, not implemented.** Written 2026-10-10 from three\n-owner rulings of that date and the codex Architect review\n+Status: **Frozen and implemented.** Written 2026-10-10 from three owner\n+rulings of that date and the codex Architect review\n `collab/1791510000000-architect-semantic-register-vm.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md`\n (PROCEED_WITH_CHANGES; its six required changes are the spine of §§3-8).\n-Subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Replaces §2, §4 and\n-§5 of [`yin.vm.semantic.md`](./yin.vm.semantic.md) when it lands; until\n-then that document describes the running machine. `semantic-register-vm`\n-is a working name: on cutover (§10 phase 8) the old evaluator is deleted\n-and this one takes the name `yin.vm.semantic`.\n+Subordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Implemented as\n+`yin.vm.semantic` (phases 3-7, 2026-10-10 to 2026-10-14; §10 records the\n+gates). Written and frozen under the working name\n+`semantic-register-vm` (this file was\n+`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`); the phase-8 cutover (2026-10-15)\n+deleted the old stack-shaped evaluator and this design and its\n+namespace took the name `yin.vm.semantic`, as §1 ruling 3 and §10\n+phase 8 said they would. Its companion designs were renamed with it:\n+`yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md`, `yin.vm.semantic.derivations.md`,\n+`yin.vm.semantic.continuation.md` and\n+`yin.vm.semantic.cursor-cell-numbering.md`.\n+\n+**History.** This file's name belonged, from 2026-09-14 to the\n+2026-10-15 cutover, to the stack-shaped semantic VM (\"`yin.vm.semantic`\n+on dao.stream — linear executable datoms\", revisions 1-2, execution\n+contracts `\"v2\"` then `\"v3\"`), which this design replaced and phase 8\n+deleted together with its kernel, loaders, lowerers and canonicalizer.\n+That document's §1 held this design's stance avant la lettre —\n+everything is a stream (instruction fetch inside a loaded segment\n+deliberately excepted), interpretation creates semantics, code and\n+state are datoms, everything is a continuation — and its table of the\n+six invariants (no hidden global state, no implicit control flow, no\n+callbacks, no shared mutable state, no layer collapsing, no assumed\n+graphs) is honored by this machine unchanged (§12). The `\"v2\"`/`\"v3\"`\n+revision history of that execution contract is published in\n+`yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` (entry I-6, 2026-10-15), which also records\n+that the old transport sources (`ucf/handoff.cljc`, versions 0-2)\n+died in 8f-1 rather than 8d; a `\"v3\"` vector is refused by stamp under\n+`\"v4\"` with no migration path (§6). Citations elsewhere of that\n+document's §2-§5 (the segment datoms, the `\"v3\"` opcode table, the\n+stack-machine CESK transitions, the flattening) are historical and\n+resolve to this paragraph or to the UCF §7.3.2 precedence note.\n \n Amended 2026-10-10 after the phase-4 design review (codex rulings F1-F7,\n text in `yin.vm.semantic.evaluator.md` §6): §2.1 (K has one\n@@ -59,7 +86,8 @@\n    `yin.vm.linker.md` §3, UCF v2 amendment §4).\n 2. The AST walker and the semantic VM are **isomorphic**: equal\n    observable behaviour on every program